00:04:37.080 --> 00:04:39.025
So lets get started with anxiety
management.

00:04:39.025 --> 00:04:44.880
85% of people tell us that they're nervous
when speaking in public.

00:04:44.880 --> 00:04:47.430
And I think the other 15% are lying.

00:04:47.430 --> 00:04:49.280
Okay?
We could create a situation where we

00:04:49.280 --> 00:04:51.040
could make them nervous too.

00:04:51.040 --> 00:04:56.780
In fact, just this past week a study from
Chapman University asked American's,

00:04:56.780 --> 00:04:58.870
what are the things you fear most?

00:04:58.870 --> 00:05:02.430
And among being caught in a surprise
terrorist attack,

00:05:02.430 --> 00:05:05.950
having identity, your identity stolen, was
public speaking.

00:05:05.950 --> 00:05:08.490
Among the top five was speaking in front
of others.

00:05:09.710 --> 00:05:13.730
This is a ubiquitous fear, and one that I
believe we can learn to manage.

00:05:13.730 --> 00:05:17.960
And I use that word manage very carefully
because I

00:05:17.960 --> 00:05:20.120
don't think we ever want to overcome it.

00:05:20.120 --> 00:05:22.050
Anxiety actually helps us.

00:05:22.050 --> 00:05:26.020
It gives us energy, helps us focus, tells
us what we're doing is important.

00:05:26.020 --> 00:05:28.090
But we want to learn to manage it.

00:05:28.090 --> 00:05:31.090
So I'd like to introduce you to a few
techniques that can work and

00:05:31.090 --> 00:05:34.340
all of these techniques are based on
academic research.

00:05:34.340 --> 00:05:39.500
But before we get there, I'd love to ask
you what does it feel like

00:05:39.500 --> 00:05:42.630
when you're sitting in the audience
watching a nervous speaker present,

00:05:42.630 --> 00:05:44.879
how do you feel, just shout out a few
things, how to do you feel?

00:05:46.110 --> 00:05:46.690
&gt;&gt; Uncomfortable.

00:05:46.690 --> 00:05:48.170
&gt;&gt; Uncomfortable.

00:05:48.170 --> 00:05:49.970
I heard many of you going, yes,
uncomfortable.

00:05:49.970 --> 00:05:52.150
It feels very awkward, doesn't it?

00:05:52.150 --> 00:05:52.870
So what do we do?

00:05:52.870 --> 00:05:55.785
Now a couple of you probably like watching
somebody suffer.

00:05:55.785 --> 00:05:58.010
[LAUGH] 'Kay, but most of us don't.

00:05:58.010 --> 00:05:58.790
So what do we do?

00:05:58.790 --> 00:06:02.830
We sit there and we nod and we smile or we
disengage.

00:06:04.140 --> 00:06:06.020
And to the nervous speaker looking out at
his or

00:06:06.020 --> 00:06:10.820
her audience seeing a bunch of people
nodding or disengaged, that does not help.

00:06:10.820 --> 00:06:12.250
Okay.
So we need to manage our anxiety.

00:06:12.250 --> 00:06:15.750
Cuz, fundamentally, your job as a
communicator rather, regardless of

00:06:15.750 --> 00:06:20.170
if it's planned or spontaneous, is to make
your audience comfortable.

00:06:20.170 --> 00:06:23.180
Because if they're comfortable they can
receive your message.

00:06:23.180 --> 00:06:26.370
And when I say comfortable I am not
referring to the fact that

00:06:26.370 --> 00:06:30.520
your message has to be sugar coated and
nice for them to hear.

00:06:30.520 --> 00:06:32.070
It can be a harsh message.

00:06:32.070 --> 00:06:35.170
But they have to be in a place where they
can receive it.

00:06:35.170 --> 00:06:40.250
So it's incumbent on you as a communicator
to help your audience feel comfortable and

00:06:40.250 --> 00:06:43.030
we do that by managing our anxiety.

00:06:43.030 --> 00:06:45.880
So let me introduce you to a few
techniques that I think you can

00:06:45.880 --> 00:06:48.670
use right away to help you feel more
comfortable.

00:06:50.310 --> 00:06:54.120
The first has to do with when you begin to
feel those anxiety symptoms.

00:06:54.120 --> 00:06:57.750
For most people this happens the, in the
initial minutes prior to speaking.

00:06:59.260 --> 00:07:02.690
In this situation what happens is many of
us begin to feel whatever it

00:07:02.690 --> 00:07:03.670
is that happens to you.

00:07:03.670 --> 00:07:05.960
Maybe your stomach gets a little gurgly.

00:07:05.960 --> 00:07:07.450
Maybe your legs begin to shake.

00:07:07.450 --> 00:07:09.580
Maybe you begin to perspire.

00:07:09.580 --> 00:07:13.630
And then we start to say to ourselves, oh,
my goodness, I'm nervous.

00:07:13.630 --> 00:07:15.360
oh.
They're gonna tell I'm nervous.

00:07:15.360 --> 00:07:16.390
This is not gonna go well.

00:07:16.390 --> 00:07:18.050
And we start spiraling out of control.

00:07:19.410 --> 00:07:23.010
So, research on mindful attention tells us
that if,

00:07:23.010 --> 00:07:25.910
when we begin to feel those anxiety
symptoms,

00:07:25.910 --> 00:07:31.130
we simply greet our anxiety and say hey,
this is me feeling nervous.

00:07:31.130 --> 00:07:33.170
I'm about to do something of consequence.

00:07:34.340 --> 00:07:37.420
And simply by greeting your anxiety and
acknowledging it,

00:07:37.420 --> 00:07:39.480
that it's normal and natural.

00:07:39.480 --> 00:07:41.460
Heck, 85% of people tell us they have it.

00:07:42.950 --> 00:07:47.030
You actually can stem the tide of that
anxiety spiraling out of control.

00:07:47.030 --> 00:07:49.740
It's not necessarily going to reduce the
anxiety but

00:07:49.740 --> 00:07:51.960
it will stop it from spinning out.

00:07:51.960 --> 00:07:55.020
So the next time you begin to feel those
anxiety signs,

00:07:55.020 --> 00:07:58.890
take a deep breath and say, this is me
feeling anxious.

00:08:00.880 --> 00:08:02.560
I notice a few of you taking some notes.

00:08:02.560 --> 00:08:04.670
There's a handout that will come at the
end.

00:08:04.670 --> 00:08:08.830
It has everything that I'm supposed to
say, okay?

00:08:08.830 --> 00:08:11.220
Can't guarantee I'm gonna say it, but
you'll have it there.

00:08:12.560 --> 00:08:15.650
In addition to this approach, a technique
that works very well, and

00:08:15.650 --> 00:08:18.760
this is a technique that I helped do some
research on way back when I was in

00:08:18.760 --> 00:08:23.990
graduate school, has to do with re-framing
how you see the speaking situation.

00:08:25.010 --> 00:08:28.120
Most of us, when we are up presenting,
planned or

00:08:28.120 --> 00:08:34.620
spontaneous, we feel that we have to do it
right and we feel like we are performing.

00:08:34.620 --> 00:08:37.230
How many of you have ever acted, done
singing or

00:08:37.230 --> 00:08:39.900
dancing, I am not going to ask for
performances now, okay.

00:08:39.900 --> 00:08:40.950
Many of you have.

00:08:40.950 --> 00:08:44.470
We should note that we could do next year,
maybe, a talent show of alums.

00:08:44.470 --> 00:08:45.850
It looks like we got the talent there.

00:08:45.850 --> 00:08:46.350
That's great.

00:08:47.910 --> 00:08:50.990
So when you perform, you know that there's
a right way and a wrong way to do it.

00:08:50.990 --> 00:08:55.730
If you don't hit your, the right note or
you right line at the right time,

00:08:55.730 --> 00:08:58.670
at the right place, you've made a mistake.

00:08:58.670 --> 00:09:00.800
It messes up the audience.

00:09:00.800 --> 00:09:03.180
It messes up the people on stage.

00:09:03.180 --> 00:09:06.540
But when you present, there is no right
way.

00:09:06.540 --> 00:09:07.910
There's certainly better and worse ways.

00:09:07.910 --> 00:09:09.680
But there is no one right way.

00:09:09.680 --> 00:09:13.410
So we need to look at presenting as
something other than performance.

00:09:13.410 --> 00:09:17.740
And what I'd like to suggest is what we
need to see this is as is a conversation.

00:09:18.780 --> 00:09:21.230
Right now, I'm having a conversation with
100 plus people.

00:09:23.080 --> 00:09:26.080
Rather than saying I'm performing for you.

00:09:26.080 --> 00:09:29.450
But it's not enough just to say, this is a
conversation.

00:09:29.450 --> 00:09:32.870
I want to give you some concrete things
you can do.

00:09:32.870 --> 00:09:36.130
First, start with questions.

00:09:36.130 --> 00:09:39.270
Questions by their very nature are
dialogic, they're two way.

00:09:39.270 --> 00:09:43.410
What was one of the very first things I
did here for you?

00:09:43.410 --> 00:09:45.840
I had you count the number of fs and raise
your hands.

00:09:45.840 --> 00:09:47.540
I asked you a question.

00:09:47.540 --> 00:09:49.180
That gets your audience involved,

00:09:49.180 --> 00:09:53.360
it makes it feel to me as the presenter as
if we're in conversation.

00:09:53.360 --> 00:09:54.360
So, use questions.

00:09:54.360 --> 00:09:55.810
They can be rhetorical.

00:09:55.810 --> 00:09:58.940
They can be polling, perhaps I actually
want to hear information from you.

00:10:00.200 --> 00:10:04.920
In fact, I use questions when I create an
outline for my presentations.

00:10:04.920 --> 00:10:08.460
Rather than writing bullet points, I list
questions that I'm going to answer.

00:10:08.460 --> 00:10:11.730
And that puts me in that conversational
mode.

00:10:11.730 --> 00:10:13.130
If you were to look at my notes for

00:10:13.130 --> 00:10:16.570
today's talk, you'll see it's just a
series of questions.

00:10:16.570 --> 00:10:20.060
Right now I'm answering the question, how
do we manage our anxiety?

00:10:21.860 --> 00:10:24.450
Beyond questions, another very useful
technique for

00:10:24.450 --> 00:10:28.950
making us conversational is to use
conversational language.

00:10:30.240 --> 00:10:33.620
Many nervous speakers distance themselves
physically.

00:10:33.620 --> 00:10:36.080
If you’ve ever seen a nervous speaker
present, he or

00:10:36.080 --> 00:10:37.650
she will say something like this.

00:10:37.650 --> 00:10:40.060
Welcome, I am really excited to be here
with you.

00:10:41.700 --> 00:10:43.630
They pull as far away from you as
possible,

00:10:43.630 --> 00:10:46.340
because you threaten us, speakers.

00:10:46.340 --> 00:10:48.660
You make us nervous so we want to get away
from you.

00:10:48.660 --> 00:10:50.960
We do the same thing linguistically.

00:10:50.960 --> 00:10:53.710
We use language that distances ourselves.

00:10:53.710 --> 00:10:56.884
It's not unusual to hear a nervous speaker
say something like,

00:10:56.884 --> 00:10:58.840
one must consider the ramifications.

00:11:00.180 --> 00:11:03.830
Or, today we're going to cover step one,
step two, step three.

00:11:03.830 --> 00:11:05.110
That's very distancing language.

00:11:06.390 --> 00:11:08.690
To be more conversational, use
conversational language.

00:11:08.690 --> 00:11:10.010
Instead of one must consider say,

00:11:10.010 --> 00:11:13.240
this is important to you, we all need to
be concerned with.

00:11:13.240 --> 00:11:16.760
Do you hear that inclusive conversational
language?

00:11:16.760 --> 00:11:17.790
Has to do with the pronouns.

00:11:19.020 --> 00:11:21.070
Instead of step 1, step, 2, step 3.

00:11:21.070 --> 00:11:26.370
First what we need to do is this, the
second thing you should consider is here.

00:11:26.370 --> 00:11:28.480
Use conversational language, so

00:11:28.480 --> 00:11:31.580
being conversational can also help you
manage your anxiety.

00:11:33.280 --> 00:11:36.930
The third technique I would like to share
is research that I actually started when I

00:11:36.930 --> 00:11:39.630
was an undergraduate here, I was very
fortunate to study with

00:11:39.630 --> 00:11:42.560
Phil Zimbardo of the Stanford Prison
experiment fame.

00:11:43.590 --> 00:11:49.080
Many people don't know that Zim actually
was instrumental in starting one of

00:11:49.080 --> 00:11:53.720
the very first shyness institutes in the,
the world and especially in the country.

00:11:53.720 --> 00:11:58.770
And I did some research with him that
looked at how your orientation to time

00:11:58.770 --> 00:12:00.480
influences how you react.

00:12:01.800 --> 00:12:05.770
And what we learned is if you can bring
yourself into the present moment,

00:12:05.770 --> 00:12:08.150
rather than being worried about the future
consequences,

00:12:09.150 --> 00:12:11.050
you can actually be less nervous.

00:12:11.050 --> 00:12:14.900
Most of us, when we present, are worried
about the future consequences.

00:12:14.900 --> 00:12:17.310
My students are worried they're not going
to get the right grade.

00:12:17.310 --> 00:12:19.300
Some of you are worried you might not get
the funding.

00:12:19.300 --> 00:12:20.600
You might not get the support.

00:12:20.600 --> 00:12:23.130
You might not get the laughs that you
want.

00:12:23.130 --> 00:12:24.980
All of those are future states.

00:12:24.980 --> 00:12:29.250
So if we can bring ourselves into the
present moment, we're not going to be

00:12:29.250 --> 00:12:32.620
as concerned about those future states and
therefore we will be less nervous.

00:12:33.840 --> 00:12:35.540
There are lots of ways to become present
oriented.

00:12:36.980 --> 00:12:41.300
I know a professional speaker, he's paid
$10,000 an hour to speak.

00:12:41.300 --> 00:12:43.830
It's a good gig.

00:12:43.830 --> 00:12:44.640
He gets very nervous.

00:12:44.640 --> 00:12:47.040
He's up in front of crowds of thousands.

00:12:47.040 --> 00:12:50.535
Behind the stage what he does is 100
push-ups right before he comes out.

00:12:50.535 --> 00:12:55.030
[LAUGH] You can't be that physically
active and not be in the present moment.

00:12:55.030 --> 00:12:58.200
Now, I'm not recommending all of us go to
that level of exertion cuz he

00:12:58.200 --> 00:13:00.225
starts out out of breath and sweaty, okay?

00:13:00.225 --> 00:13:04.070
[LAUGH] But a walk around the building
before you speak.

00:13:04.070 --> 00:13:05.890
That can do it.
There are other ways.

00:13:05.890 --> 00:13:08.130
If you've ever watched athletes perform
and

00:13:08.130 --> 00:13:10.660
get ready to do their event, they listen
to music.

00:13:10.660 --> 00:13:14.070
They focus on a song or a playlist that
helps get them in the moment.

00:13:15.220 --> 00:13:17.570
You can do things as simple as counting
backwards from 100

00:13:17.570 --> 00:13:18.430
by tough number like 17.

00:13:18.430 --> 00:13:23.500
I'm gonna pause 'cuz I know people in the
room are trying.

00:13:23.500 --> 00:13:24.460
Yeah.

00:13:24.460 --> 00:13:27.080
Get's hard after that third or fourth one,
I know.

00:13:27.080 --> 00:13:30.460
My favorite way to get present-oriented is
to say tongue twisters.

00:13:31.900 --> 00:13:34.160
Saying a tongue twister forces you to be
in the moment.

00:13:34.160 --> 00:13:35.780
Otherwise you'll say it wrong.

00:13:35.780 --> 00:13:38.450
And it has the added benefit of warming up
your voice.

00:13:38.450 --> 00:13:41.930
Most nervous speakers don't warm up their
voice.

00:13:41.930 --> 00:13:45.400
They retreat inside themselves and start
saying all these bad things to themselves.

00:13:45.400 --> 00:13:48.820
So, saying a tongue twister can help you
be both present-oriented and

00:13:48.820 --> 00:13:49.410
warm up your voice.

00:13:50.930 --> 00:13:53.680
Remember, I said today we're gonna have a
lot of participation?

00:13:53.680 --> 00:13:57.210
I'm gonna ask you to repeat after me my
favorite tongue twister, and

00:13:57.210 --> 00:14:01.710
I like this tongue twister because if you
say it wrong you say a naughty word, and

00:14:01.710 --> 00:14:04.980
I'm gonna be listening to see if I hear
any naughty words this morning.

00:14:04.980 --> 00:14:05.840
Okay?

00:14:05.840 --> 00:14:06.440
Repeat after me.

00:14:06.440 --> 00:14:07.830
It's only three phrases.

00:14:07.830 --> 00:14:09.150
I slit a sheet.

00:14:11.810 --> 00:14:12.910
A sheet I slit.

00:14:14.770 --> 00:14:17.820
And on that slitted sheet I sit.

00:14:21.780 --> 00:14:23.110
Very good, no shits.

00:14:23.110 --> 00:14:23.610
Excellent.

00:14:24.640 --> 00:14:25.140
Very good.

00:14:26.390 --> 00:14:31.440
Now in that moment, in that moment, you
weren't worried about,

00:14:31.440 --> 00:14:35.070
I'm in front of all these people, this is
weird, this guy's having me do this.

00:14:35.070 --> 00:14:37.730
You were so focused on saying it right and

00:14:37.730 --> 00:14:41.220
trying to figure out what the naughty word
was that you were in the present moment.

00:14:41.220 --> 00:14:41.990
That's how easy it is.

00:14:43.450 --> 00:14:47.080
So it's very possible for us to manage our
anxiety.

00:14:48.120 --> 00:14:52.815
We can do it initially by greeting the
anxiety when we begin to feel those signs.

00:14:52.815 --> 00:14:57.519
We can do it when we re-frame the
situation as a conversation.

00:14:57.519 --> 00:15:00.583
And we do it when we become present
oriented.

00:15:00.583 --> 00:15:04.510
Those are three of many tools that exist
to help you manage your anxiety.

00:15:05.870 --> 00:15:09.200
If you have questions about other ways,
I'm happy to chat with you.

00:15:09.200 --> 00:15:12.140
And at the end, I'm gonna point you to
some resources that you can

00:15:12.140 --> 00:15:15.120
refer to to help you find additional
sources for you.

00:15:17.260 --> 00:15:20.730
So let's get started on the core part of
what we're doing today,

00:15:20.730 --> 00:15:25.420
which is how to feel more comfortable
speaking in spontaneous situations.

00:15:25.420 --> 00:15:27.040
Some very simple ground rules for you.

00:15:28.690 --> 00:15:32.830
First, I'm going to identify four steps
that I believe are critical to

00:15:32.830 --> 00:15:36.970
becoming effective at speaking in, in a
spontaneous situation.

00:15:36.970 --> 00:15:40.530
With each of those steps, I'm going to ask
you to participate in an activity.

00:15:40.530 --> 00:15:44.130
None of them are more painful than saying
the tongue twister out loud.

00:15:44.130 --> 00:15:46.810
They may require you to stand up, they
might require you to talk to

00:15:46.810 --> 00:15:49.900
the person next to you, but none of them
are painful.

00:15:49.900 --> 00:15:52.860
And then finally, I'm going to conclude
with a phrase or

00:15:52.860 --> 00:15:56.020
saying that comes from the wonderful world
of improvisation.

00:15:57.770 --> 00:16:00.490
Through the continuing studies program
here at Stanford, for

00:16:00.490 --> 00:16:03.930
the past five years, I have co-taught a
class with Adam Tobin.

00:16:03.930 --> 00:16:08.300
He is a lecturer in the Creative Arts
Department.

00:16:08.300 --> 00:16:10.510
He teaches film and new media.

00:16:10.510 --> 00:16:11.630
And he's an expert at improv.

00:16:11.630 --> 00:16:14.390
And we've partnered together to help
people learn how to

00:16:14.390 --> 00:16:16.230
speak more spontaneously.

00:16:16.230 --> 00:16:18.760
We call it improvisationally speaking.

00:16:18.760 --> 00:16:21.360
And Adam has taught me wonderful phrases
and

00:16:21.360 --> 00:16:25.020
ideas from improv that I want to impart to
you, that really stick.

00:16:25.020 --> 00:16:28.280
That's why I'm sharing them with you, to
help you remember these techniques.

00:16:28.280 --> 00:16:31.870
And again at the end of all this, you'll
get a handout that has this listed.

00:16:31.870 --> 00:16:33.525
So let's get started.

00:16:33.525 --> 00:16:39.966
The very first thing that gets in people's
way when it comes to spontaneous speaking,

00:16:39.966 --> 00:16:41.211
is themselves.

00:16:41.211 --> 00:16:43.858
We get in our own way.

00:16:43.858 --> 00:16:45.808
We want to be perfect.

00:16:45.808 --> 00:16:48.037
We want to give the right answer.

00:16:48.037 --> 00:16:51.600
We want out toast to be incredibly
memorable.

00:16:51.600 --> 00:16:56.420
These things are burdened by our effort,
by our trying.

00:16:56.420 --> 00:16:58.080
The best thing we can do,

00:16:58.080 --> 00:17:02.730
the first step in our process, is to get
ourselves out of the way.

00:17:04.150 --> 00:17:05.970
Easier said than done.

00:17:05.970 --> 00:17:09.677
Most of us in this room are in this room
because we are type A personalities.

00:17:09.677 --> 00:17:13.927
We work hard, we think fast, we make sure
that we get things right.

00:17:13.927 --> 00:17:18.530
But that can actually serve as a
disservice as we try to

00:17:18.530 --> 00:17:20.381
speak in the moment.

00:17:20.381 --> 00:17:23.841
I'd like to demonstrate a little of this
for you, and I need your help to do that.

00:17:23.841 --> 00:17:26.604
So we're going to do our first activity.

00:17:26.604 --> 00:17:31.016
We are going to do an activity that's
called shout the wrong name.

00:17:31.016 --> 00:17:36.040
In a moment, if you are able and willing,
I'm going to ask you to stand.

00:17:36.040 --> 00:17:40.380
And I am going to ask you, for about 30
seconds, to look all around you in

00:17:40.380 --> 00:17:43.100
this environment, and you are going to
point at different things.

00:17:43.100 --> 00:17:46.210
And I know it's rude to point, but for
this exercise, please point.

00:17:46.210 --> 00:17:49.180
I want you to point to things, and you are
going to call the things you

00:17:49.180 --> 00:17:52.470
are pointing to, out loud, anything but
what they really are.

00:17:54.090 --> 00:17:56.330
So I might point to this and say,
refrigerator.

00:17:57.620 --> 00:17:59.850
I might point to this and say, cat.

00:18:01.070 --> 00:18:03.720
I am pointing to anything in your
environment around you.

00:18:03.720 --> 00:18:06.370
It can be the person sitting next to you,
standing next to you.

00:18:06.370 --> 00:18:10.750
You will just shout, and shouting is
important, the wrong name.

00:18:13.570 --> 00:18:16.727
So in a moment I'm gonna ask you to stand
and do that.

00:18:16.727 --> 00:18:19.324
Please raise your hand if you already have
the first five or

00:18:19.324 --> 00:18:21.023
six things you're going to call out.

00:18:21.023 --> 00:18:22.950
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:18:22.950 --> 00:18:26.178
&gt;&gt; Yeah, that's what I'm talking about.

00:18:26.178 --> 00:18:28.058
We stockpile.

00:18:28.058 --> 00:18:30.560
You all are excellent gameplayers.

00:18:30.560 --> 00:18:33.740
I told you the game, shout the wrong name.

00:18:33.740 --> 00:18:37.440
And you have already begun figuring out
how you're going to master the game.

00:18:37.440 --> 00:18:41.982
That's your brain trying to help you get
it right.

00:18:41.982 --> 00:18:43.344
I'd like to suggest,

00:18:43.344 --> 00:18:48.089
the only way you can get this activity
wrong is by doing what you've just done.

00:18:48.089 --> 00:18:49.226
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:18:49.226 --> 00:18:51.235
&gt;&gt; There is no way to get this wrong.

00:18:51.235 --> 00:18:53.080
Okay.

00:18:53.080 --> 00:18:58.465
Even if I call this a chair, no penalty
will be bestowed upon you.

00:18:58.465 --> 00:18:59.390
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:18:59.390 --> 00:19:00.155
&gt;&gt; Okay?

00:19:00.155 --> 00:19:01.560
Because I won't know what you were
pointing at.

00:19:01.560 --> 00:19:04.630
You could have been pointing at the floor
under the chair, and

00:19:04.630 --> 00:19:06.150
you called the floor the chair and you
were fine.

00:19:07.240 --> 00:19:11.160
The point is, we are planning and working
to get it right.

00:19:11.160 --> 00:19:13.310
And there is no way to get it right.

00:19:13.310 --> 00:19:15.240
Just doing it gets it right.

00:19:15.240 --> 00:19:16.890
Okay, so let's try this now.

00:19:16.890 --> 00:19:18.250
We're gonna play this game twice.

00:19:18.250 --> 00:19:19.510
Again, it's for 30 seconds.

00:19:19.510 --> 00:19:22.020
If you are willing and able, will you
please stand up?

00:19:22.020 --> 00:19:23.078
You can do this seated, by the way.

00:19:23.078 --> 00:19:25.006
But if you're willing and able, let's
stand up.

00:19:25.006 --> 00:19:28.210
Okay, in a moment I am about to say, go.

00:19:28.210 --> 00:19:32.419
And I would like for you to point at
anything around here, including me.

00:19:32.419 --> 00:19:33.557
It's okay to point at me.

00:19:33.557 --> 00:19:36.041
I hope it's not a bad thing you say when
you point at me.

00:19:36.041 --> 00:19:39.386
But point at different things, and loudly
and

00:19:39.386 --> 00:19:42.914
proudly call them different than what they
are.

00:19:42.914 --> 00:19:43.959
Ready?

00:19:43.959 --> 00:19:44.957
Begin.

00:19:44.957 --> 00:19:46.304
&gt;&gt; [CROSSTALK].

00:19:46.304 --> 00:19:47.279
&gt;&gt; Porcupine.

00:19:47.279 --> 00:19:48.440
&gt;&gt; [CROSSTALK].

00:19:48.440 --> 00:19:56.423
&gt;&gt; California, salt shaker, car, library,

00:19:56.423 --> 00:20:04.415
tennis racket, purple, orange, putrid.

00:20:04.415 --> 00:20:05.924
Hello.

00:20:05.924 --> 00:20:12.750
[NOISE]
Time, time.

00:20:12.750 --> 00:20:13.751
[NOISE] Let's,

00:20:13.751 --> 00:20:18.532
you can stay standing, cuz in mere
moments, we're going to do it again.

00:20:18.532 --> 00:20:20.459
So if you're comfortable standing, we're
about to do it again.

00:20:20.459 --> 00:20:21.666
First, thank you.

00:20:21.666 --> 00:20:22.642
That was wonderful.

00:20:22.642 --> 00:20:24.910
I heard great words being called out.

00:20:24.910 --> 00:20:25.930
It was, it was fun.

00:20:25.930 --> 00:20:27.820
And some of you in the back were doing it
in sync.

00:20:27.820 --> 00:20:29.803
So it looked like you were doing some 70s
disco dance.

00:20:29.803 --> 00:20:30.616
It was awesome.

00:20:30.616 --> 00:20:31.429
Okay.

00:20:31.429 --> 00:20:33.147
This, this was great.

00:20:33.147 --> 00:20:36.240
Now, let me ask you just a few questions.

00:20:36.240 --> 00:20:39.380
Did you notice anything about the words
that you were saying?

00:20:40.980 --> 00:20:43.650
Did we find patterns, perhaps?

00:20:43.650 --> 00:20:46.680
Maybe some of you were going through
fruits and vegetables.

00:20:46.680 --> 00:20:52.040
A few of you were going through things
that started with the letter A, right?

00:20:52.040 --> 00:20:55.229
That's your brain saying, okay you told me
not to stockpile, so

00:20:55.229 --> 00:21:00.729
I'm gonna try to be a little more devious
and I'm gonna give you patterns, okay?

00:21:00.729 --> 00:21:03.670
Same problem.

00:21:03.670 --> 00:21:07.100
When we teach that class I told you about,
that improvisationally speaking class,

00:21:07.100 --> 00:21:09.290
we like to say, your brain is there to
help you.

00:21:10.390 --> 00:21:14.060
These things it's doing have helped you be
successful, but like a windshield wiper,

00:21:14.060 --> 00:21:17.700
we just wanna wipe those suggestions away
and see what happens.

00:21:17.700 --> 00:21:19.080
Okay.

00:21:19.080 --> 00:21:20.760
So we're going to do this activity again.

00:21:22.000 --> 00:21:26.210
This time, try the best you can to thank
your brain if it provides you

00:21:26.210 --> 00:21:29.450
with patterns or stockpiles and just say
thank you brain.

00:21:29.450 --> 00:21:30.810
And disregard them.

00:21:30.810 --> 00:21:33.760
Okay, so lets see what happens when we're
not stockpiling and

00:21:33.760 --> 00:21:34.930
we're not playing off patterns.

00:21:34.930 --> 00:21:39.130
We'll do this for only 15 seconds, see how
this feels.

00:21:40.130 --> 00:21:41.260
Baby steps.

00:21:41.260 --> 00:21:47.336
Ready begin.
[NOISE] Kodak, [NOISE] Bicycle chain.

00:21:47.336 --> 00:21:49.193
Skate board.

00:21:49.193 --> 00:21:53.530
Bananas.

00:21:53.530 --> 00:21:57.571
Purple.

00:21:57.571 --> 00:21:59.004
Putrid.

00:21:59.004 --> 00:22:04.089
[NOISE]
Time.

00:22:04.089 --> 00:22:05.575
Please have a seat.

00:22:05.575 --> 00:22:07.920
Thank you again.

00:22:07.920 --> 00:22:13.960
Did you notice a difference between the
second time and the first time.

00:22:15.060 --> 00:22:18.230
Yes, was it a little easier that second
time?

00:22:18.230 --> 00:22:18.730
No.

00:22:20.080 --> 00:22:21.240
That's okay.

00:22:21.240 --> 00:22:22.900
We're just starting.

00:22:22.900 --> 00:22:25.055
These skills are not like a light switch.

00:22:25.055 --> 00:22:26.920
It's not like you learn these [UNKNOWN]
skills and

00:22:26.920 --> 00:22:29.520
then all of a sudden you can execute on
them.

00:22:29.520 --> 00:22:30.540
This is a wonderful game.

00:22:30.540 --> 00:22:36.520
This is a wonderful game to train your
brain to get out of its own way.

00:22:36.520 --> 00:22:39.040
You can play this game anywhere, anytime.

00:22:39.040 --> 00:22:41.055
I like to play this game when I'm sitting
in traffic.

00:22:41.055 --> 00:22:42.340
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:22:42.340 --> 00:22:45.300
&gt;&gt; Makes me feel better than the, I shout
things out.

00:22:45.300 --> 00:22:47.410
They're not the naughty things that I
wanna be shouting out.

00:22:47.410 --> 00:22:49.840
But I shout out things, and it helps.

00:22:49.840 --> 00:22:51.550
You're training yourself to get out of
your own way.

00:22:51.550 --> 00:22:55.830
You're working against the muscle memory
that you've developed over the course of

00:22:55.830 --> 00:22:59.150
your life with a vain, a brain that acts
very fast to help you solve problems.

00:22:59.150 --> 00:23:01.710
But in essence, in spontaneous speaking
situations,

00:23:01.710 --> 00:23:06.340
you put too much pressure on yourself
trying to figure out how to get it right.

00:23:08.170 --> 00:23:10.910
So a game like this teaches us to get out
of our own way.

00:23:10.910 --> 00:23:17.470
It teaches us to see the things that we do
that prevent us from acting spontaneously.

00:23:19.350 --> 00:23:23.530
In essence we are reacting rather than
responding.

00:23:23.530 --> 00:23:24.740
To react means to act again.

00:23:26.750 --> 00:23:28.160
You've thought it and now you're acting on
it.

00:23:28.160 --> 00:23:30.080
That takes too long and it's too
thoughtful.

00:23:30.080 --> 00:23:34.380
We want to respond in a way that's genuine
and authentic.

00:23:36.158 --> 00:23:38.920
So the maxims I would like for you to take
from this, and

00:23:38.920 --> 00:23:42.670
again these maxims come from
improvisation, is one of my favorite.

00:23:42.670 --> 00:23:43.870
Dare to be dull.

00:23:45.120 --> 00:23:49.190
And in a room like this, telling you dare
to be dull is offensive, and I apologize.

00:23:49.190 --> 00:23:51.420
But this will help.

00:23:51.420 --> 00:23:56.650
Rather than stre, striving for greatness,
dare to be dull.

00:23:57.660 --> 00:24:00.070
And if you dare to be dull and allow
yourself that,

00:24:00.070 --> 00:24:02.640
you will reach that greatness.

00:24:02.640 --> 00:24:05.860
It's when you set greatness as your
target,

00:24:05.860 --> 00:24:08.830
that it gets in the way of you ever
getting there.

00:24:08.830 --> 00:24:10.890
Because you over evaluate, you over
analyze, you freeze up.

00:24:12.140 --> 00:24:17.580
So the first step in our process today, is
to get out of our own way.

00:24:17.580 --> 00:24:19.100
Dare to be dull.

00:24:19.100 --> 00:24:20.080
Easier said than done.

00:24:20.080 --> 00:24:21.700
But once you practice,

00:24:21.700 --> 00:24:25.389
and a game just as simple as the one we
practiced, is a great way to do it.

00:24:27.060 --> 00:24:28.770
But that's not enough.

00:24:28.770 --> 00:24:30.720
Getting out of our own way is important.

00:24:30.720 --> 00:24:34.690
But the second step of our process has us
change how we

00:24:34.690 --> 00:24:36.740
see the situation we find ourselves in.

00:24:37.910 --> 00:24:43.600
We need to see the speaking opportunity
that we are a part of as an opportunity,

00:24:43.600 --> 00:24:45.030
rather than a challenge and a threat.

00:24:46.580 --> 00:24:48.950
When I coach executives on Q and

00:24:48.950 --> 00:24:55.870
A skills, when they go in front of the
media or whatever, investors.

00:24:55.870 --> 00:24:58.350
They see it as an adversarial experience,
me versus them.

00:24:58.350 --> 00:25:04.400
And one of the first things I work on is
change the way you approach it.

00:25:06.190 --> 00:25:09.250
A Q and A session, for example, is an
opportunity for you.

00:25:09.250 --> 00:25:10.530
It's an opportunity to clarify,

00:25:10.530 --> 00:25:13.260
it's an opportunity to understand what
people are thinking.

00:25:13.260 --> 00:25:16.920
So if we look at it as an opportunity, it
feels very different.

00:25:18.030 --> 00:25:22.190
We see it differently, and therefore we
have more freedom to respond.

00:25:22.190 --> 00:25:24.150
When I feel that you are challenging me,

00:25:24.150 --> 00:25:28.360
I am going to do the bare minimum to
respond and protect myself.

00:25:29.490 --> 00:25:32.720
If I see this as an opportunity where I
have a chance to explain and

00:25:32.720 --> 00:25:37.200
expand, I'm going to interact differently
with you.

00:25:37.200 --> 00:25:40.340
So, spontaneous speaking situations are
ones that afford you opportunities.

00:25:42.460 --> 00:25:44.710
So when you're at a corporate dinner, and
your boss turns to you and

00:25:44.710 --> 00:25:46.620
says, oh, you know him better than the
rest.

00:25:46.620 --> 00:25:48.170
Would you mind introducing him?

00:25:48.170 --> 00:25:53.340
You say, great, thank you for the
opportunity, rather than, [UNKNOWN] right?

00:25:53.340 --> 00:25:54.380
I better get this right.

00:25:55.850 --> 00:25:58.530
So see things as an opportunity.

00:25:58.530 --> 00:26:02.250
I have a game to play to help us with
this.

00:26:03.250 --> 00:26:07.260
This is a fun one, the holidays are
approaching, we all, in this room,

00:26:07.260 --> 00:26:08.820
are going to give and receive gifts.

00:26:09.980 --> 00:26:11.910
Here is how this game will work.

00:26:11.910 --> 00:26:14.180
It works best if you have a partner.

00:26:14.180 --> 00:26:17.000
So I am hoping you can work with somebody
sitting next to you.

00:26:17.000 --> 00:26:18.330
If there is nobody sitting next to you,

00:26:18.330 --> 00:26:21.310
turn around, introduce yourself, great way
to connect.

00:26:21.310 --> 00:26:22.950
If not, you can play this game by
yourself.

00:26:22.950 --> 00:26:25.440
It's just a little harder, and you can't
do the second part of the game.

00:26:25.440 --> 00:26:29.040
So, after I explain the game, give, this
gives you a chance to,

00:26:29.040 --> 00:26:30.400
to get to know somebody.

00:26:30.400 --> 00:26:31.260
Here's how it works.

00:26:31.260 --> 00:26:33.340
If you have a partner, you and

00:26:33.340 --> 00:26:37.280
your partner are going to exchange
imaginary gifts, okay?

00:26:37.280 --> 00:26:38.540
Pretend you have a gift.

00:26:38.540 --> 00:26:41.440
It can be a big gift, can be a small gift.

00:26:41.440 --> 00:26:42.940
And you will give your gift to your
partner.

00:26:44.780 --> 00:26:47.910
Your partner will take the gift and open
it up and

00:26:47.910 --> 00:26:51.870
will tell you what you gave them, because
you have no, you just gave them a gift.

00:26:51.870 --> 00:26:55.000
So you are going to open up the box, and
you're going to look inside.

00:26:55.000 --> 00:26:58.260
And you are going to say the first thing
that comes to your mind in the moment,

00:26:58.260 --> 00:27:01.714
not the thing you have all just thought
of.

00:27:01.714 --> 00:27:03.780
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Or the thing after that.

00:27:03.780 --> 00:27:05.310
Remember what we talked about before?

00:27:05.310 --> 00:27:07.750
That still plays, that's still in play.

00:27:07.750 --> 00:27:09.530
Okay, you're stock piling.

00:27:09.530 --> 00:27:10.860
Look in there.

00:27:10.860 --> 00:27:14.330
My favorite that I said, somebody gave me
this, a gift during playing this game,

00:27:14.330 --> 00:27:17.240
I looked inside and I saw a frog leg.

00:27:17.240 --> 00:27:20.060
I don't know why I saw a frog leg, but
that's what I said.

00:27:20.060 --> 00:27:21.720
That's the first part of the activity.

00:27:22.750 --> 00:27:26.950
Now, the opportunity is twofold in this
game.

00:27:26.950 --> 00:27:29.910
The opportunity is for you, the gift
receiver, to name a gift.

00:27:29.910 --> 00:27:30.550
That's kind of fun.

00:27:30.550 --> 00:27:31.370
That's an opportunity.

00:27:31.370 --> 00:27:32.020
It's not a threat.

00:27:33.310 --> 00:27:34.680
But the real opportunity is for

00:27:34.680 --> 00:27:38.020
the gift giver, because the gift giver
then has to say.

00:27:39.490 --> 00:27:41.710
So you look and you say thank you for
giving me a frog's leg, and

00:27:41.710 --> 00:27:47.450
the person will, will look at you and say
I knew you wanted a frog's leg, because So

00:27:47.450 --> 00:27:52.580
whatever you find the person who has
received it is going to say absolutely,

00:27:52.580 --> 00:27:54.990
I'm so glad you're happy, I got it for you
because.

00:27:54.990 --> 00:27:57.510
So you have to respond to whatever they
say.

00:27:58.630 --> 00:27:59.890
Right?

00:27:59.890 --> 00:28:00.930
What a great opportunity.

00:28:00.930 --> 00:28:02.660
Now some of you are sitting there going,
oh, that's hard.

00:28:02.660 --> 00:28:04.000
I don't wanna do it, I might make a fool
out of myself.

00:28:04.000 --> 00:28:05.970
Others of you are, if you're following
this advice,

00:28:05.970 --> 00:28:08.800
are saying, what a great opportunity.

00:28:08.800 --> 00:28:09.380
Right?

00:28:09.380 --> 00:28:11.050
So, the game again is played like this.

00:28:11.050 --> 00:28:14.080
You and your partner will exchange, each
will exchange a gift.

00:28:14.080 --> 00:28:16.280
One will start, then the other will
follow.

00:28:16.280 --> 00:28:18.340
The first person will give a gift to the
second person.

00:28:18.340 --> 00:28:21.930
Second person opens the box, however big
the box is, and if the box is big, and

00:28:21.930 --> 00:28:24.740
you find a penny in it, perfect, doesn't
matter.

00:28:24.740 --> 00:28:26.970
The box is heavy and you find a feather in
it, fine.

00:28:26.970 --> 00:28:28.770
It doesn't, there's no way to get it
wrong.

00:28:28.770 --> 00:28:29.310
Okay?

00:28:29.310 --> 00:28:30.630
Whatever's in the box is in the box.

00:28:30.630 --> 00:28:32.750
You can return it and get what you wanted
later.

00:28:32.750 --> 00:28:33.663
Okay?
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:28:33.663 --> 00:28:37.640
&gt;&gt; The person, then, you will name it.

00:28:37.640 --> 00:28:40.750
You will say thank you for the, whatever
you saw in the box.

00:28:40.750 --> 00:28:44.880
The person who gave it to you will say,
I'm so glad you're excited.

00:28:44.880 --> 00:28:46.290
I got it for you because.

00:28:46.290 --> 00:28:52.080
And you will give a reason that you got
them whatever they decided you gave them.

00:28:52.080 --> 00:28:53.230
Make sense?

00:28:53.230 --> 00:28:53.950
All right.

00:28:53.950 --> 00:28:55.070
So, very quickly just,

00:28:55.070 --> 00:28:57.880
in five seconds, find a partner if you're
willing to do this with a partner.

00:28:57.880 --> 00:28:58.765
Everybody have a partner?

00:28:58.765 --> 00:28:59.673
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:28:59.673 --> 00:29:00.532
&gt;&gt; Okay.

00:29:00.532 --> 00:29:05.095
[BLANK_AUDIO]

00:29:05.095 --> 00:29:07.657
All right.
In your partnerships,

00:29:07.657 --> 00:29:12.540
in your partnerships, pick an a person and
a b person.

00:29:12.540 --> 00:29:14.870
You may stand or sit, it's totally up to
you.

00:29:16.940 --> 00:29:17.680
Pick an a and pick a b.

00:29:17.680 --> 00:29:18.180
Okay?

00:29:20.480 --> 00:29:23.484
B goes first, [LAUGH].

00:29:23.484 --> 00:29:26.399
All right.

00:29:26.399 --> 00:29:29.070
B, give a a gift.

00:29:29.070 --> 00:29:34.530
A thank them, and then b will name and
give the reason they gave it to them.

00:29:34.530 --> 00:29:40.403
[NOISE]
If

00:29:40.403 --> 00:29:48.793
you have not

00:29:48.793 --> 00:29:57.183
switched,

00:29:57.183 --> 00:30:02.217
switch

00:30:02.217 --> 00:30:08.930
please.

00:30:08.930 --> 00:30:11.333
If you have not switched, switch please.

00:30:11.333 --> 00:30:19.435
[NOISE]
Let's

00:30:19.435 --> 00:30:25.327
wrap it up

00:30:25.327 --> 00:30:34.902
in 30 seconds

00:30:34.902 --> 00:30:40.797
please.

00:30:40.797 --> 00:30:41.555
Let's wrap it up.

00:30:41.555 --> 00:30:45.106
[NOISE]

00:30:45.106 --> 00:30:52.208
All right.

00:30:52.208 --> 00:30:55.843
If we can all have our seats.

00:30:55.843 --> 00:31:04.587
[NOISE] If we can all [NOISE] take our
seats please.

00:31:04.587 --> 00:31:13.997
[NOISE] I know I'm telling a room of many
MBA alums to stop talking and that's hard.

00:31:13.997 --> 00:31:22.105
[NOISE] All right, ladies and gentlemen.

00:31:22.105 --> 00:31:23.895
Did you get what you wanted?

00:31:23.895 --> 00:31:25.320
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:31:25.320 --> 00:31:26.190
&gt;&gt; Pretty neat, huh?

00:31:26.190 --> 00:31:28.550
You always get what you want.

00:31:28.550 --> 00:31:32.650
Now for some of you this was really hard
because you, you're really taking

00:31:32.650 --> 00:31:36.410
the challenge and, and not seeing what was
in the box until you looked in there.

00:31:36.410 --> 00:31:38.760
Okay.
Was anybody surprised by what you

00:31:38.760 --> 00:31:39.320
found in the box?

00:31:40.840 --> 00:31:42.270
What did you find sir, what was in the
box?

00:31:46.170 --> 00:31:46.740
What?

00:31:46.740 --> 00:31:47.890
Oh, wow!

00:31:47.890 --> 00:31:49.380
Nice!

00:31:49.380 --> 00:31:51.680
Nice, if you've got a Ferrari you need a
transmission.

00:31:51.680 --> 00:31:52.610
I like it.

00:31:52.610 --> 00:31:54.870
Who else found something that was
surprising?

00:31:56.460 --> 00:31:57.120
What did you find?

00:31:57.120 --> 00:31:57.750
A live unicorn!

00:31:57.750 --> 00:31:59.450
That's a great gift.

00:31:59.450 --> 00:32:00.800
Right?

00:32:00.800 --> 00:32:02.770
How was it as the gift giver?

00:32:02.770 --> 00:32:06.110
Were you surprised at what your partner
found in the box?

00:32:06.110 --> 00:32:09.260
Isn't it interesting that when we give an
imaginary gift knowing that

00:32:09.260 --> 00:32:12.980
the person's gonna name it we already have
in mind what they're gonna find?

00:32:12.980 --> 00:32:16.130
And when they say live unicorn, we go well
that's interesting.

00:32:16.130 --> 00:32:17.260
Right?

00:32:17.260 --> 00:32:22.430
So the point of this game is, to one,

00:32:22.430 --> 00:32:24.300
remind ourselves we have to get out of our
own way,

00:32:24.300 --> 00:32:28.620
like we talked about before, but to see
this as an opportunity and to have fun.

00:32:28.620 --> 00:32:31.460
I love watching people play this game.

00:32:31.460 --> 00:32:35.820
The number of smiles that I saw amongst
you and, and I have to admit when I

00:32:35.820 --> 00:32:39.125
first started some of you looked a little
dour, a little doubting, okay?

00:32:39.125 --> 00:32:40.600
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].
&gt;&gt; But in that last game you all

00:32:40.600 --> 00:32:42.740
were smiling and looked like you were
having fun, so

00:32:42.740 --> 00:32:48.300
when you reframe the spontaneous speaking
opportunity as, as an opportunity,

00:32:48.300 --> 00:32:51.850
as something that you co-create and share.

00:32:51.850 --> 00:32:55.930
All the sudden, you are less nervous, less
defensive,

00:32:55.930 --> 00:32:58.570
and you can accomplish something pretty
darn good.

00:32:58.570 --> 00:33:00.410
In this case, a fun outcome.

00:33:01.840 --> 00:33:06.670
This reminds us of perhaps the most famous
of all improvisation sayings,.

00:33:06.670 --> 00:33:08.160
Yes and.

00:33:08.160 --> 00:33:11.810
A lot of us live our communication lives
saying no but.

00:33:13.350 --> 00:33:16.590
Yes and opens up a tremendous amount of
opportunities.

00:33:16.590 --> 00:33:20.300
And this doesn't mean you have to say yes
and to a question somebody asks.

00:33:20.300 --> 00:33:22.610
This just means the approach you take to
the situation.

00:33:22.610 --> 00:33:27.050
So you're going to ask me questions,
that's an opportunity.

00:33:27.050 --> 00:33:32.380
Yes, and I will follow through, versus no
and being defensive.

00:33:32.380 --> 00:33:36.180
So, we've accomplished the first two steps
of our process.

00:33:36.180 --> 00:33:37.568
First we get out of our own way,

00:33:37.568 --> 00:33:40.980
[UNKNOWN] we can reframe the situation as
an opportunity.

00:33:42.750 --> 00:33:45.990
The next phase is also hard, but

00:33:45.990 --> 00:33:50.510
very rewarding, and that is to slow down,
and listen.

00:33:51.990 --> 00:33:55.450
You need to understand the demands of the
requirement you find yourself in,

00:33:56.480 --> 00:33:59.190
in order to respond appropriately.

00:33:59.190 --> 00:34:01.480
But often, we jump ahead.

00:34:01.480 --> 00:34:04.220
We listen just enough to think we got it,

00:34:04.220 --> 00:34:07.250
and then we go ahead, starting [UNKNOWN]
to think about.

00:34:07.250 --> 00:34:09.550
What we're gonna respond and then we
respond.

00:34:09.550 --> 00:34:11.030
We really need to listen.

00:34:11.030 --> 00:34:13.190
Because fundamentally, as a communicator,

00:34:13.190 --> 00:34:15.590
your job is to be in service of your
audience.

00:34:15.590 --> 00:34:17.740
And if you don't understand what your
audience is asking or

00:34:17.740 --> 00:34:20.610
needs, you can't fulfill that obligation.

00:34:20.610 --> 00:34:23.010
So we need to slow down and listen.

00:34:23.010 --> 00:34:25.735
I have a fun game to play.

00:34:25.735 --> 00:34:35.051
[SOUND] In this game you are going to
S-P-E-L-L E-V-E-R-Y-T-H-I-N-G

00:34:35.051 --> 00:34:41.671
Y-O-U S-A-Y T-O Y-O-U-R P-A-R-T-N-E-R.

00:34:41.671 --> 00:34:43.806
[BLANK_AUDIO]

00:34:43.806 --> 00:34:46.963
I will translate.
You're going to get with the same partner

00:34:46.963 --> 00:34:49.400
you just worked with.

00:34:49.400 --> 00:34:53.010
And you are going to have a very brief
conversation about something fun that you

00:34:53.010 --> 00:34:54.750
plan to do today.

00:34:54.750 --> 00:34:56.640
I know this is the most fun you are going
to have all day.

00:34:56.640 --> 00:34:58.330
But the next fun thing you are going to do
today.

00:34:58.330 --> 00:35:01.010
You are going to tell your partner what
you are going to do that will

00:35:01.010 --> 00:35:01.620
be fun today.

00:35:01.620 --> 00:35:06.831
But you are going to do so by
S-P-E-L-L-I-N-G I-T.

00:35:06.831 --> 00:35:08.731
[BLANK_AUDIO]

00:35:08.731 --> 00:35:10.150
Okay?

00:35:10.150 --> 00:35:11.230
So you're going to spell it.

00:35:12.870 --> 00:35:14.535
It's okay if you are not a good speller.

00:35:14.535 --> 00:35:18.050
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; 'Kay?

00:35:18.050 --> 00:35:19.980
Look I, you'll see the benefit of doing
this.

00:35:19.980 --> 00:35:24.950
So, with the partner you just worked with,
person a is going to go first this time.

00:35:24.950 --> 00:35:26.830
You are simply going to tell your partner.

00:35:26.830 --> 00:35:31.790
Actually you're going to spell to your
partner, what it is of fun,

00:35:31.790 --> 00:35:34.350
something of fun, that you're going to do
today.

00:35:34.350 --> 00:35:35.250
Okay?

00:35:35.250 --> 00:35:36.440
Do what you were really going to do for

00:35:36.440 --> 00:35:40.482
fun and not do things like F-E-E-D T-H-E
C-A-T, right,

00:35:40.482 --> 00:35:42.320
just cuz you don't wanna spell, right?

00:35:42.320 --> 00:35:44.830
So, you can use big words.

00:35:44.830 --> 00:35:46.920
Alright, 30 seconds each.

00:35:46.920 --> 00:35:49.639
Spell to your partner something fun that
you're going to do today.

00:35:49.639 --> 00:35:54.944
[BLANK_AUDIO]

00:35:54.944 --> 00:35:55.828
Would you like to play?

00:35:55.828 --> 00:35:58.882
[NOISE] Go ahead.

00:35:58.882 --> 00:36:00.290
&gt;&gt; G-O-T A-T G-A-M-E.
&gt;&gt; Oh my goodness say it again.

00:36:00.290 --> 00:36:01.570
Spell it again.

00:36:01.570 --> 00:36:02.369
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:36:02.369 --> 00:36:06.398
&gt;&gt; G-O-T A-T G-A-M-E.

00:36:06.398 --> 00:36:10.927
&gt;&gt; E-X-C-E-L-L-E-N-T.

00:36:10.927 --> 00:36:16.165
I H-O-P-E T-H-A-T T-H-E-Y W-I-N.

00:36:16.165 --> 00:36:20.168
&gt;&gt; E-X-C-E-L-L-E-N-T.

00:36:20.168 --> 00:36:20.870
&gt;&gt; Thank you.

00:36:20.870 --> 00:36:22.205
That was very good.

00:36:22.205 --> 00:36:23.573
Thank you.

00:36:23.573 --> 00:36:33.573
[BLANK_AUDIO]

00:36:43.826 --> 00:36:47.130
[NOISE] If you have not switched, switch.

00:36:47.130 --> 00:36:49.956
Take 30 more seconds with the new partner
spelling.

00:36:49.956 --> 00:36:53.192
[NOISE]

00:36:53.192 --> 00:37:01.606
G-R-E-A-T

00:37:01.606 --> 00:37:08.725
exclamation

00:37:08.725 --> 00:37:13.257
point.

00:37:13.257 --> 00:37:17.180
T-H-A-N-K-Y-O-U.

00:37:17.180 --> 00:37:20.696
P-L-E-A-S-E.

00:37:20.696 --> 00:37:27.312
T-A-K-E Y-O-U-R S-E-A-T.

00:37:27.312 --> 00:37:33.543
[NOISE] So what did we learn?

00:37:33.543 --> 00:37:34.880
What did we learn?

00:37:34.880 --> 00:37:36.735
Besides that we're not so good at
spelling.

00:37:36.735 --> 00:37:41.610
&gt;&gt; [INAUDIBLE]
&gt;&gt; You have to pause between the words.

00:37:43.490 --> 00:37:48.470
How did this change your interaction with
the person you were interacting with?

00:37:48.470 --> 00:37:50.110
What did you have to do?

00:37:50.110 --> 00:37:52.240
&gt;&gt; Focus.
&gt;&gt; Focus, and listen.

00:37:53.380 --> 00:37:55.290
And you can't be thinking ahead.

00:37:55.290 --> 00:37:57.260
You have to be in the moment.

00:37:57.260 --> 00:38:02.030
When you listen and truly understand what
the person is trying to say,

00:38:02.030 --> 00:38:05.330
then you can respond in a better way, a
more targeted response.

00:38:06.650 --> 00:38:07.820
We often don't listen.

00:38:09.530 --> 00:38:12.490
So we start by getting out of our own way.

00:38:13.780 --> 00:38:17.160
We then reframe the situation as an
opportunity.

00:38:17.160 --> 00:38:19.220
Those are things we do inside our head.

00:38:19.220 --> 00:38:21.850
But in the moment of interacting, we have
to listen first,

00:38:21.850 --> 00:38:24.150
before we can respond to the spontaneous
request.

00:38:25.740 --> 00:38:30.350
Perhaps my most favorite maxim comes from
this activity.

00:38:31.450 --> 00:38:33.269
Don't just do something, stand there.

00:38:33.269 --> 00:38:35.505
[BLANK_AUDIO]

00:38:35.505 --> 00:38:36.180
Listen.

00:38:37.860 --> 00:38:40.050
Listen, and then respond.

00:38:42.370 --> 00:38:43.670
Now, how do we respond?

00:38:43.670 --> 00:38:45.870
That brings us to the fourth part of our
process.

00:38:47.190 --> 00:38:50.400
And that is, we have to tell a story.

00:38:50.400 --> 00:38:52.730
We respond in a way that has a structure.

00:38:52.730 --> 00:38:54.080
All stories have structure.

00:38:54.080 --> 00:38:56.380
We have to respond in a structured way.

00:38:57.430 --> 00:39:00.410
The key to successful spontaneous speaking
and

00:39:00.410 --> 00:39:02.660
by the way planned speaking is having a
structure.

00:39:04.110 --> 00:39:09.040
I would like to introduce you to two of
the most prevalent and popular and useful

00:39:09.040 --> 00:39:14.110
structures you can use to communicate a
message in a spontaneous situation.

00:39:14.110 --> 00:39:17.650
But before we get there, we have to talk
about the value of structure.

00:39:17.650 --> 00:39:21.250
It increases what is called processing
fluency, the effectiveness of which, or

00:39:21.250 --> 00:39:24.100
through which we process information.

00:39:25.460 --> 00:39:28.920
We actually process structured
information, roughly 40% more

00:39:28.920 --> 00:39:32.690
effectively and efficiently than
information that's not structured.

00:39:33.780 --> 00:39:37.800
I love looking out in this audience,
because you will remember as I remember.

00:39:37.800 --> 00:39:38.670
Phone numbers.

00:39:38.670 --> 00:39:41.820
When you had to remember them if you
wanted to call somebody.

00:39:41.820 --> 00:39:42.780
Okay.

00:39:42.780 --> 00:39:45.220
Young folks today don't need to remember
phone numbers.

00:39:45.220 --> 00:39:47.060
They just need to look at a picture, push
a button and

00:39:47.060 --> 00:39:49.710
then the voice starts talking on the other
end.

00:39:49.710 --> 00:39:52.640
Ten digit phone numbers, it's actually
hard to remember ten digit phone numbers.

00:39:52.640 --> 00:39:53.960
How did you do it?

00:39:53.960 --> 00:39:55.420
You chunked it into a structure.

00:39:55.420 --> 00:39:57.390
Three, three and four.

00:39:57.390 --> 00:39:58.600
Structure helps us remember.

00:40:00.170 --> 00:40:04.190
The same is true when speaking
spontaneously or in a planned situation.

00:40:04.190 --> 00:40:06.780
So let me introduce you to two useful
structures.

00:40:06.780 --> 00:40:08.980
The first useful structure you have
probably heard or

00:40:08.980 --> 00:40:14.250
used in some incarnation, it is the
problem, solution, benefit, structure.

00:40:14.250 --> 00:40:16.490
You start by talking about what the issue
is, the problem.

00:40:18.340 --> 00:40:19.890
You then talk about a way of solving it,

00:40:19.890 --> 00:40:22.440
and then you talk about the benefits of
following through on it.

00:40:22.440 --> 00:40:24.920
Very persuasive, very effective.

00:40:24.920 --> 00:40:26.410
Helps you as the speaker remember it,

00:40:26.410 --> 00:40:27.970
helps your audience know where you're
going with it.

00:40:29.510 --> 00:40:33.020
When I was a tour guide on this campus,
many, many, many years ago,.

00:40:34.740 --> 00:40:36.850
What do you think the single most
important thing they

00:40:36.850 --> 00:40:37.710
drilled into our head?

00:40:37.710 --> 00:40:41.590
It took a full quarter, by the way, to
train to be a tour guide here.

00:40:41.590 --> 00:40:43.850
They used to line us up at one end of the
quad, and

00:40:43.850 --> 00:40:47.720
have us walk backward, straight, and if
you failed you had to start over.

00:40:47.720 --> 00:40:51.490
To this day, I can walk backwards in a
straight like because of that.

00:40:51.490 --> 00:40:52.380
As part of that training,

00:40:52.380 --> 00:40:54.700
what do you think the most important thing
they taught us was?

00:40:56.860 --> 00:40:58.925
Never lose your tour group.

00:40:58.925 --> 00:41:02.360
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I'm not joking.

00:41:02.360 --> 00:41:04.160
Never, that's, never lose your tour group.

00:41:05.590 --> 00:41:07.430
The same is true as a presenter.

00:41:07.430 --> 00:41:09.330
Never lose your audience.

00:41:09.330 --> 00:41:12.630
The way you keep your audience on track is
by providing structure.

00:41:12.630 --> 00:41:15.388
None of you would go on a tour with me if
I said, hi, my name's Matt.

00:41:15.388 --> 00:41:18.067
Let's go.
[BLANK_AUDIO]

00:41:18.067 --> 00:41:20.146
You wanna know where you're going, why
you're going there,

00:41:20.146 --> 00:41:21.060
how long it's gonna take?

00:41:21.060 --> 00:41:24.440
You need to set expectations and structure
does that.

00:41:24.440 --> 00:41:27.990
Problem, solution, benefit is a wonderful
structure to have in your back pocket.

00:41:29.460 --> 00:41:32.330
It's something that you can use quickly
when you're in the moment.

00:41:34.140 --> 00:41:36.780
It can be reframed so it's not always a
problem you're talking about.

00:41:36.780 --> 00:41:37.910
Maybe it's an opportunity.

00:41:37.910 --> 00:41:40.670
Maybe there's a market opportunity you
wanna go out and capture.

00:41:40.670 --> 00:41:42.120
It's not a problem that we're not doing
it.

00:41:42.120 --> 00:41:44.350
But maybe we'd be better off if we did.

00:41:44.350 --> 00:41:48.660
So it becomes opportunity, solution, which
are the steps to achieve it.

00:41:48.660 --> 00:41:49.230
And then the benefit.

00:41:51.060 --> 00:41:52.970
Another structure.

00:41:52.970 --> 00:41:56.890
Which works equally, equally well, is the
what?

00:41:56.890 --> 00:41:57.970
So what?

00:41:57.970 --> 00:41:58.570
Now what?

00:41:58.570 --> 00:41:59.830
Structure.

00:41:59.830 --> 00:42:05.540
You start by talking about what it is,
then you talk about why it's important,

00:42:05.540 --> 00:42:06.880
and then what the next steps are.

00:42:08.980 --> 00:42:12.110
This is a wonderful formula for answering
questions.

00:42:13.130 --> 00:42:14.880
For introducing people.

00:42:14.880 --> 00:42:17.290
So if, in the moment somebody asks me to
introduce somebody,

00:42:17.290 --> 00:42:18.940
I change the what to who.

00:42:18.940 --> 00:42:21.720
I say who they are, why they're important
and what we're gonna do next.

00:42:21.720 --> 00:42:23.560
Maybe listen to them, maybe drink our
wine, whatever.

00:42:23.560 --> 00:42:24.060
All right.

00:42:25.410 --> 00:42:26.990
What, so what, now what.

00:42:26.990 --> 00:42:29.940
The reality is this, when you are in
spontaneously speaking situation,

00:42:29.940 --> 00:42:31.730
you have to do two things simultaneously.

00:42:31.730 --> 00:42:35.300
You have to figure out what to say and how
to say it.

00:42:35.300 --> 00:42:38.890
These structures help you by telling you
how to say it.

00:42:41.910 --> 00:42:45.010
If you can become comfortable with these
structures, you can be

00:42:45.010 --> 00:42:49.800
in a situation where you can respond very
ably to spontaneous speaking situations.

00:42:49.800 --> 00:42:52.380
We're gonna practice.

00:42:52.380 --> 00:42:53.040
Cuz that's what we do.

00:42:54.200 --> 00:42:55.140
Here's the situation.

00:42:55.140 --> 00:42:56.880
Is everybody familiar with this child's
toy?

00:42:56.880 --> 00:42:58.390
It's a slinky.

00:42:58.390 --> 00:42:59.870
Okay?

00:42:59.870 --> 00:43:04.980
You are going to sell this slinky to your
partner using either problem,

00:43:04.980 --> 00:43:08.640
solution, benefit or opportunity,
solution, benefit.

00:43:08.640 --> 00:43:10.180
What does the slinky provide you?

00:43:12.130 --> 00:43:13.980
Or you could use what, so what, now what?

00:43:13.980 --> 00:43:15.330
What is it?
Why is it important?

00:43:15.330 --> 00:43:17.560
And the next steps might be to buy it,
okay?

00:43:17.560 --> 00:43:21.700
So by using that structure, see how
already it helps you?

00:43:21.700 --> 00:43:22.531
It helps you focus.

00:43:22.531 --> 00:43:24.542
Get with your partner and we,

00:43:24.542 --> 00:43:29.438
we're only going to have one partner sell
to the other partner, okay?

00:43:29.438 --> 00:43:31.186
So get with your partner.

00:43:32.200 --> 00:43:35.040
One of you will volunteer to sell to the
other, okay?

00:43:35.040 --> 00:43:39.378
Sell a slinky using problem, solution,
benefit or what so what, now what?

00:43:39.378 --> 00:43:44.430
Please begin.

00:43:44.430 --> 00:43:54.430
[NOISE].

00:43:57.460 --> 00:43:58.940
&gt;&gt; So we have the handouts, but I'm also
going to be doing the, the-

00:43:58.940 --> 00:43:59.620
&gt;&gt; The microphone?

00:43:59.620 --> 00:44:04.110
&gt;&gt; Mic. So. &gt;&gt; When I debrief this, you
can go ahead and pass them out.

00:44:04.110 --> 00:44:05.580
Does that make sense?

00:44:05.580 --> 00:44:06.738
&gt;&gt; Okay, so after, after.

00:44:06.738 --> 00:44:09.670
&gt;&gt; No no, mm-hm, after this activity.

00:44:09.670 --> 00:44:10.340
&gt;&gt; Okay. And then. &gt;&gt; And then.

00:44:10.340 --> 00:44:10.887
&gt;&gt; After that.

00:44:10.887 --> 00:44:20.719
&gt;&gt; [NOISE]

00:44:20.719 --> 00:44:28.585
30

00:44:28.585 --> 00:44:36.451
more

00:44:36.451 --> 00:44:46.451
seconds,

00:44:54.149 --> 00:45:04.149
please.

00:45:09.882 --> 00:45:13.987
[NOISE]

00:45:13.987 --> 00:45:23.021
Excellent.

00:45:23.021 --> 00:45:26.029
Let's all close the deal, seal the deal.

00:45:26.029 --> 00:45:33.470
[NOISE] I have never seen.

00:45:33.470 --> 00:45:36.185
More people in one place doing this at the
same time.

00:45:36.185 --> 00:45:39.020
[LAUGH]
I love it.

00:45:39.020 --> 00:45:41.360
I teach people to gesture and gesture big,
it's great.

00:45:41.360 --> 00:45:42.750
I love it.

00:45:42.750 --> 00:45:47.470
So if you were the recipient of the sales
pitch, thumbs up.

00:45:47.470 --> 00:45:48.230
Did they do a good job?

00:45:49.590 --> 00:45:51.640
Did they use the structure?

00:45:51.640 --> 00:45:52.350
Awesome.

00:45:52.350 --> 00:45:54.805
I'm recruiting you all for my next
business as my salespeople.

00:45:54.805 --> 00:45:57.390
[LAUGH] Please try to ignore this,

00:45:57.390 --> 00:46:01.940
but as we're speaking the handout I told
you about is coming around.

00:46:01.940 --> 00:46:03.235
On the back of that handout,

00:46:03.235 --> 00:46:07.250
you're going to see a list of structures,
the two we talked about and

00:46:07.250 --> 00:46:10.650
several others, that can help you in
spontaneous speaking situations.

00:46:11.670 --> 00:46:13.740
These structures help.

00:46:13.740 --> 00:46:17.470
Because they help you understand, how
you're going to say what you say.

00:46:17.470 --> 00:46:21.220
Structure sets you free and I know that's
kind of ironic, but it's true, if

00:46:21.220 --> 00:46:25.060
you have that structure then you are free
to think about what you are going to say.

00:46:26.330 --> 00:46:29.950
It reduces the cognitive load of figuring
out what you are saying, and

00:46:29.950 --> 00:46:31.500
how you are going to say it.

00:46:31.500 --> 00:46:33.510
All of this is on that handout, okay?

00:46:35.680 --> 00:46:37.790
So what does this all mean?

00:46:37.790 --> 00:46:43.290
It means that we have, within our ability,
the tools and

00:46:43.290 --> 00:46:47.150
the approaches, to help us in spontaneous
speaking situations.

00:46:47.150 --> 00:46:50.380
The very first thing we have to do is
manage our anxiety,

00:46:50.380 --> 00:46:52.820
because you can't be an effective speaker.

00:46:52.820 --> 00:46:55.610
If you don't have your anxiety under
control.

00:46:55.610 --> 00:46:59.220
And we talked about how you can do that by
greeting your anxiety,

00:46:59.220 --> 00:47:03.810
reframing as a conversation, and being in
the present moment.

00:47:03.810 --> 00:47:07.500
Once you do that, you need to practice a
series of four steps,

00:47:07.500 --> 00:47:10.340
that will help you speak spontaneously.

00:47:10.340 --> 00:47:12.660
First you get out of your own way.

00:47:12.660 --> 00:47:15.910
I would love it if all of you, on your way
from here to the football game,

00:47:17.000 --> 00:47:18.675
point at things and call them the wrong
name.

00:47:18.675 --> 00:47:20.600
[LAUGH] It'll be fun.

00:47:20.600 --> 00:47:22.490
If most of us do it, then it won't be
weird.

00:47:22.490 --> 00:47:24.350
If only one and two of us do it, it'll be
weird.

00:47:24.350 --> 00:47:24.860
Right.

00:47:24.860 --> 00:47:26.290
Second.

00:47:26.290 --> 00:47:27.950
Give gifts.

00:47:27.950 --> 00:47:31.940
By that I mean see your interactions as
ones of opportunity, not challenges.

00:47:33.930 --> 00:47:38.070
Third, take the time to listen, listen.

00:47:39.740 --> 00:47:42.120
And then finally, use structures.

00:47:42.120 --> 00:47:43.920
And you have to practice these structures.

00:47:43.920 --> 00:47:46.100
I practice these structures on my kids.

00:47:46.100 --> 00:47:46.780
I have two kids.

00:47:46.780 --> 00:47:49.860
When they ask me questions, I usually
answer them in what, so what, now what.

00:47:50.910 --> 00:47:52.470
They don't know it.

00:47:52.470 --> 00:47:54.170
But, when they go over to their friends'
houses and

00:47:54.170 --> 00:47:56.940
they see their friends ask their dads
questions, they don't get what, so

00:47:56.940 --> 00:47:57.540
what, now what.

00:47:57.540 --> 00:48:00.080
So, you know, you have to practice.

00:48:00.080 --> 00:48:02.140
The more you practice, the more
comfortable you will become.

00:48:03.670 --> 00:48:07.700
Ultimately you have the opportunity before
you to become more compelling,

00:48:07.700 --> 00:48:12.330
more confident, more connected as a
speaker, if you leverage these techniques.

00:48:14.390 --> 00:48:17.365
If you're interested in learning more,
this is where I do a little plug.

00:48:17.365 --> 00:48:18.580
'Kay, I've written a book.

00:48:18.580 --> 00:48:21.610
Many of the MBA students who take the
strategic communication classes,

00:48:21.610 --> 00:48:23.390
here that I and others teach read it.

00:48:23.390 --> 00:48:25.030
It's called SPEAKING UP WITHOUT FREAKING
OUT.

00:48:26.610 --> 00:48:27.190
More importantly,

00:48:27.190 --> 00:48:30.525
there's a website here that I curate
called NOFREAKINGSPEAKING.COM.

00:48:31.550 --> 00:48:33.880
And it has lots of information that I've
written, and

00:48:33.880 --> 00:48:36.680
others have written about how to become
more effective at speaking.

00:48:36.680 --> 00:48:38.210
So that's, that's the end of my plug.

00:48:38.210 --> 00:48:44.150
What I'd really like to do is, enter into
a spontaneous speaking situation with you.

00:48:44.150 --> 00:48:46.620
And I would love to entertain any
questions that you have.

00:48:46.620 --> 00:48:49.420
There are two people who are running
around with microphones, so

00:48:49.420 --> 00:48:52.100
some of us re, who remember the Phil
Donahue show.

00:48:52.100 --> 00:48:53.984
We're gonna do a little bit of that.

00:48:53.984 --> 00:48:57.591
If you have a question, the microphone
will come, and I'm happy to answer it.

00:48:57.591 --> 00:48:58.428
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:48:58.428 --> 00:48:59.832
&gt;&gt; I think if you-

00:48:59.832 --> 00:49:00.471
&gt;&gt; Is it on?

00:49:00.471 --> 00:49:01.561
&gt;&gt; Yep, yep.

00:49:01.561 --> 00:49:02.409
[LAUGH] We can hear you.

00:49:02.409 --> 00:49:02.925
&gt;&gt; Great.

00:49:02.925 --> 00:49:05.823
Can you talk about hostile situations?

00:49:05.823 --> 00:49:06.844
&gt;&gt; Hostile situations, yes.

00:49:06.844 --> 00:49:09.909
So when you find yourself in a challenging
situation.

00:49:09.909 --> 00:49:12.950
First, It should not be a surprise to you.

00:49:12.950 --> 00:49:14.483
It should not be a surprise.

00:49:14.483 --> 00:49:15.628
Before you ever speak,

00:49:15.628 --> 00:49:18.910
you should think about what is the
environment going to be like?

00:49:18.910 --> 00:49:22.730
So it shouldn't surprise you that there
might be some challenges in the room.

00:49:24.330 --> 00:49:28.350
When there are hostile situations that
arise, you have to acknowledge it.

00:49:28.350 --> 00:49:33.120
So if somebody says, that's a ridiculous
idea, why did you come up with that?

00:49:33.120 --> 00:49:35.820
To simply say, so, the idea I came up with
was, right?

00:49:35.820 --> 00:49:40.740
Acknowledge the emotion, I recommend not
naming the emotion, right?

00:49:40.740 --> 00:49:43.470
So, you sound really angry, the person's,
I'm not angry, I'm frustrated.

00:49:43.470 --> 00:49:45.160
Now we're arguing over their mental state,
right?

00:49:45.160 --> 00:49:46.240
Emotional state.

00:49:46.240 --> 00:49:49.860
So, so I say something like, I hear you
have a lot of passion on this issue, or,

00:49:49.860 --> 00:49:51.360
I hear there's great concern from you.

00:49:51.360 --> 00:49:54.860
So you acknowledge the emotion, cuz
otherwise it sits in the room.

00:49:54.860 --> 00:49:57.910
And then reframe and respond the way that
makes sense.

00:49:57.910 --> 00:50:02.010
So if somebody raises their hand and says,
your product is ridiculously priced.

00:50:02.010 --> 00:50:03.060
Why do you charge so much?

00:50:04.680 --> 00:50:08.650
I might say I hear great concern, and what
you're really asking about is the value of

00:50:08.650 --> 00:50:11.140
our product, and I would give my value
proposition, and

00:50:11.140 --> 00:50:14.310
then I would come back and say, and
because of the value we provide.

00:50:14.310 --> 00:50:16.170
We believe it's priced fairly.

00:50:16.170 --> 00:50:17.420
So you answer the question about price,

00:50:17.420 --> 00:50:22.300
but you've reframe it in a way that you
feel more comfortable answering it.

00:50:22.300 --> 00:50:27.980
So, th, the way to do this is to practice
all the skills we just talked about.

00:50:27.980 --> 00:50:31.600
The only skill that I'm adding to this is
the awareness in

00:50:31.600 --> 00:50:34.210
advance that you might be in that
situation.

00:50:34.210 --> 00:50:37.130
First I have to truly listen to what I'm
hearing, right?

00:50:37.130 --> 00:50:39.650
It's very easy for me when I hear a
challenging question,

00:50:39.650 --> 00:50:43.140
to get all defensive and not hear what the
person's asking.

00:50:43.140 --> 00:50:45.880
I see it as an opportunity to reframe and
explain.

00:50:45.880 --> 00:50:48.880
Okay so, again, you have to practice.

00:50:48.880 --> 00:50:50.340
But, that's how I think you address it.

00:50:50.340 --> 00:50:51.670
Are there other questions?

00:50:51.670 --> 00:50:53.110
I see a question back here, yes, please.

00:50:53.110 --> 00:50:54.170
&gt;&gt; Yes, first of all, thank you very much.

00:50:54.170 --> 00:50:55.510
Great, great presentation.

00:50:55.510 --> 00:50:58.840
&gt;&gt; Thank you.
&gt;&gt; For a lot of the the speaking I do,

00:50:58.840 --> 00:51:02.620
I have remote audiences, audiences
distributed all over the country,

00:51:02.620 --> 00:51:04.030
with telecom.

00:51:04.030 --> 00:51:05.870
Any tips for those kinds of audiences?

00:51:05.870 --> 00:51:11.030
&gt;&gt; So when you are speaking in a situation
where not everybody is co-located, okay?

00:51:11.030 --> 00:51:12.500
In fact, at this very moment,

00:51:12.500 --> 00:51:15.040
there are people watching this
presentation remotely.

00:51:16.690 --> 00:51:19.710
What you need to do is be mindful of it.

00:51:19.710 --> 00:51:24.600
Second, try to include engagement
techniques where the audience actually has

00:51:24.600 --> 00:51:26.560
to do something.

00:51:26.560 --> 00:51:30.340
So, physical participation is what we did
here with the games.

00:51:30.340 --> 00:51:32.500
You can ask your audience to imagine
something,

00:51:32.500 --> 00:51:36.420
imagine what it would be like if, when we
try to achieve a goal, rather than say

00:51:36.420 --> 00:51:39.790
here is the goal we are trying to achieve,
say imagine what it would be like if.

00:51:39.790 --> 00:51:43.480
See what that does to you, it pulls you
in, I can take polling questions, most of

00:51:43.480 --> 00:51:47.220
the technology that you are referring to
has some kind of polling feature.

00:51:47.220 --> 00:51:51.910
You can open up some kind of Wiki or
Google Doc, or some collaborative tool.

00:51:51.910 --> 00:51:53.750
Where people can be doing things and

00:51:53.750 --> 00:51:56.750
you can be monitoring that while you're
presenting.

00:51:56.750 --> 00:51:58.110
So I might take some breaks.

00:51:58.110 --> 00:52:01.090
I talk for ten, 15 minutes and say, okay,
let's apply this and

00:52:01.090 --> 00:52:04.800
let's go into this Google Doc I've
created, and I see what people are doing.

00:52:04.800 --> 00:52:07.960
So it's about variety and it's about
engagement.

00:52:07.960 --> 00:52:12.030
Those are the ways that you really connect
to people who are remote from you.

00:52:12.030 --> 00:52:13.105
Okay, other questions?

00:52:13.105 --> 00:52:17.390
Who, you're pointing oh, [LAUGH], I've got
to look for where the mic is.

00:52:17.390 --> 00:52:18.530
&gt;&gt; This may be similar to

00:52:18.530 --> 00:52:19.440
the first question,
&gt;&gt; Sure.

00:52:19.440 --> 00:52:22.200
&gt;&gt; But I do a lot of expert witness
testimony.

00:52:22.200 --> 00:52:25.342
What's your recommendation for handling
cross-examination?

00:52:25.342 --> 00:52:27.620
[LAUGH] Specifically, specifically a
hostile one.

00:52:27.620 --> 00:52:29.253
&gt;&gt; I feel like I'm being cross-examined.

00:52:29.253 --> 00:52:30.294
&gt;&gt; It's very hostile.

00:52:30.294 --> 00:52:31.720
[LAUGH].

00:52:31.720 --> 00:52:36.390
In any speaking situation that you go into
that has some planned element to it I

00:52:36.390 --> 00:52:39.970
recommend identifying certain themes, that
you think are important or

00:52:39.970 --> 00:52:40.910
believe need to come out.

00:52:40.910 --> 00:52:43.660
And then with each one of those themes
have some examples and

00:52:43.660 --> 00:52:45.450
concrete evidence that you can use to
support it.

00:52:46.900 --> 00:52:50.910
You don't go in with memorized terms, or
ways of saying it.

00:52:50.910 --> 00:52:54.580
You just have ideas and themes, and then
you put them together as necessary.

00:52:54.580 --> 00:52:58.110
So, when I'm in a situation where people
are interrogating me.

00:52:58.110 --> 00:53:00.700
I have certain themes that I wanna get
across, and make sure that I,

00:53:00.700 --> 00:53:04.690
I can do that in a way that fits the needs
in the moment.

00:53:04.690 --> 00:53:10.040
If it's hostile, again, you, the, the
single best tool you have to

00:53:10.040 --> 00:53:14.610
buy yourself time and to help you answer a
question efficiently is paraphrasing.

00:53:14.610 --> 00:53:18.170
The paraphrase is like the Swiss Army
knife of communication.

00:53:18.170 --> 00:53:22.030
If you remember the show MacGyver, it's
your MacGyver tool, right?

00:53:22.030 --> 00:53:24.240
So when a question comes in.

00:53:24.240 --> 00:53:28.940
The way you paraphrase it allows you the
opportunity to reframe it,

00:53:28.940 --> 00:53:33.800
to think about your answer and, to pause
and make sure you got it right.

00:53:33.800 --> 00:53:36.500
So when you're under those situations, if
you have an opportunity to paraphrase it,

00:53:36.500 --> 00:53:39.330
say, so what you're really asking about is
x, y and z.

00:53:39.330 --> 00:53:43.360
That gives you the opportunity to employ
one of these techniques.

00:53:43.360 --> 00:53:47.500
Now I've never been an expert witness, cuz
I'm not an expert on anything, but.

00:53:47.500 --> 00:53:52.550
Those tools I believe could be helpful.

00:53:52.550 --> 00:53:54.070
The microphone is back there.

00:53:54.070 --> 00:53:54.970
Thank you.
&gt;&gt; Thank you so much.

00:53:54.970 --> 00:53:57.850
This has been so helpful and enjoyable
this morning.

00:53:57.850 --> 00:54:01.000
Would you please show the last screen, so

00:54:01.000 --> 00:54:03.270
we can get down the name of the book you
have written and the information?

00:54:03.270 --> 00:54:03.905
&gt;&gt; Absolutely.
&gt;&gt; Thank you.

00:54:03.905 --> 00:54:07.510
[LAUGH] I think they actually, you might
even have an opportunity,

00:54:07.510 --> 00:54:10.000
it's on the sheet too, everything I said
is on the back of that sheet, but

00:54:10.000 --> 00:54:12.515
I am happy to have this behind me while I
talk.

00:54:12.515 --> 00:54:14.540
[LAUGH] Other questions?

00:54:14.540 --> 00:54:15.540
Yes please?

00:54:15.540 --> 00:54:19.860
&gt;&gt; Yes, I work with groups that, from,

00:54:19.860 --> 00:54:23.030
that represent many different cultural
backgrounds.

00:54:23.030 --> 00:54:23.780
&gt;&gt; Yes.
&gt;&gt; So

00:54:23.780 --> 00:54:27.990
are there any caveats or is this a
universal strategy.

00:54:29.420 --> 00:54:30.830
&gt;&gt; So in terms of,

00:54:30.830 --> 00:54:33.160
from your perspective as the speaker,
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:54:33.160 --> 00:54:34.590
&gt;&gt; I believe this applies.

00:54:34.590 --> 00:54:38.420
But when you, whenever you communicate,
part of the listening aspect is

00:54:38.420 --> 00:54:42.720
also thinking about and is who is my
audience and what are their expectations?

00:54:42.720 --> 00:54:47.000
So what are the cultural expectations of
the audience that, I'm presenting to?

00:54:47.000 --> 00:54:50.110
So there might be certain norms and rules
that are expected.

00:54:50.110 --> 00:54:51.240
So when I travel and

00:54:51.240 --> 00:54:55.550
do talks I have to take into account where
I'm doing the, the presentations.

00:54:55.550 --> 00:54:57.900
So I, I'm, I help present in the Ignite
program.

00:54:57.900 --> 00:55:03.360
If you have not heard about the Ignite
program here at the GSB it's fantastic.

00:55:03.360 --> 00:55:07.600
And I just did a presentation standing in
one of these awesome classrooms that have

00:55:07.600 --> 00:55:10.730
all these cameras and I just taught 35
people in Santiago Chile.

00:55:12.780 --> 00:55:18.780
And I needed to understand the cultural
expectations of that area.

00:55:18.780 --> 00:55:19.790
And what they expect and

00:55:19.790 --> 00:55:21.920
what they're willing to do, when I ask
them to participate.

00:55:21.920 --> 00:55:25.440
So, it, it's part of that listening step
where you reflect on what

00:55:25.440 --> 00:55:27.840
are the expectations of the audience.

00:55:27.840 --> 00:55:29.520
I think we have time for two more
questions and

00:55:29.520 --> 00:55:32.190
then I'm gonna hang around afterwards if
anybody has individual questions.

00:55:32.190 --> 00:55:34.910
But, some of these folks really want me to
keep on schedule.

00:55:34.910 --> 00:55:36.220
Yes, please?
&gt;&gt; I wanted to ask you a question.

00:55:36.220 --> 00:55:38.550
One of the things that you've done
effectively in your talking.

00:55:38.550 --> 00:55:41.005
And I've seen other effective speakers do,
is interject humor.

00:55:41.005 --> 00:55:41.640
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.

00:55:41.640 --> 00:55:42.210
&gt;&gt; In their talk.

00:55:42.210 --> 00:55:45.740
How, what are the risks and rewards of
trying to do that.

00:55:45.740 --> 00:55:48.590
&gt;&gt; Well first, thank you, and I appreciate
all of you laughing.

00:55:48.590 --> 00:55:51.195
Those are, that's the sum total of all my
jokes, you've heard them,

00:55:51.195 --> 00:55:52.645
I'm not funny beyond those jokes.

00:55:52.645 --> 00:55:56.800
[LAUGH] So humor is wonderfully
connecting.

00:55:56.800 --> 00:55:58.770
It's wonderfully connecting, it's a great
tool for

00:55:58.770 --> 00:56:00.570
connection, it is very, very risky.

00:56:01.770 --> 00:56:03.720
Cultural reasons get in the way,

00:56:03.720 --> 00:56:07.300
sometimes what you think is funny isn't
funny to other people.

00:56:07.300 --> 00:56:10.485
What research tells us is that if you're
going to try to be funny,

00:56:10.485 --> 00:56:14.730
self-deprecating humor is your best bet,
okay?

00:56:14.730 --> 00:56:19.230
Because it is the least risky, there is
nothing worse than putting out a joke and

00:56:19.230 --> 00:56:21.250
having no response.

00:56:21.250 --> 00:56:24.920
It actually sets you back farther than if
you would have gotten,

00:56:24.920 --> 00:56:28.480
where you would have gotten if the joke
would have hit, so

00:56:28.480 --> 00:56:30.660
basic fundamentals you need to think about
with humor.

00:56:30.660 --> 00:56:34.370
One, is it funny, how do I know, I ask
other people first.

00:56:34.370 --> 00:56:37.100
Second, what happens if it doesn't work?

00:56:37.100 --> 00:56:39.850
Have a backup plan, right?

00:56:39.850 --> 00:56:42.470
And then third, if you're worried about
the answers to those first two,

00:56:42.470 --> 00:56:44.710
don't do it, right?

00:56:44.710 --> 00:56:45.890
One last question please.

00:56:45.890 --> 00:56:46.890
The microphone is right here.

00:56:46.890 --> 00:56:48.810
And then like I said, I will hang around
afterwards.

00:56:48.810 --> 00:56:49.880
Yes, please.

00:56:49.880 --> 00:56:53.905
&gt;&gt; I I'm sort of on the opposite side of
this, since I'm a journalist.

00:56:53.905 --> 00:56:55.990
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.
&gt;&gt; And I frequently have to ask

00:56:55.990 --> 00:57:03.740
spontaneous questions of people, who have
been through media training.

00:57:03.740 --> 00:57:04.265
&gt;&gt; Yes.
&gt;&gt; So.

00:57:04.265 --> 00:57:05.250
[LAUGH] So any tips for

00:57:05.250 --> 00:57:08.635
chinks in the armor, way to ask.

00:57:08.635 --> 00:57:09.490
[LAUGH]

00:57:09.490 --> 00:57:12.370
&gt;&gt; Ask a question without being
antagonistic, but

00:57:12.370 --> 00:57:15.300
get a facsimile of a straight answer.

00:57:15.300 --> 00:57:16.620
&gt;&gt; Well, so let me give you two answers.

00:57:16.620 --> 00:57:20.930
One is I I have young boys, and the power
of the why is great.

00:57:20.930 --> 00:57:22.830
Just ask why a couple times, and

00:57:22.830 --> 00:57:25.505
and you can get through that first two
layers of training.

00:57:25.505 --> 00:57:27.180
[LAUGH] You know, why do you say that?

00:57:27.180 --> 00:57:28.910
How do you feel about that?

00:57:28.910 --> 00:57:30.740
the, the second bit is.

00:57:30.740 --> 00:57:31.240
To.

00:57:32.600 --> 00:57:34.990
What I have found successful in getting
people to.

00:57:34.990 --> 00:57:38.030
I do this to get people to answer in a
more authentic way.

00:57:38.030 --> 00:57:40.810
What I'll do is I'll ask them to give
advice.

00:57:40.810 --> 00:57:43.420
So what advice would you give somebody
who's challenged with this?

00:57:43.420 --> 00:57:46.480
Or what advice would you give to somebody
in this situation?

00:57:46.480 --> 00:57:47.560
And by asking for

00:57:47.560 --> 00:57:52.350
the advice, it changes the relationship
they have to me as the question asker.

00:57:52.350 --> 00:57:54.840
And I often get much more rich detailed
information.

00:57:54.840 --> 00:57:56.310
So the power of the why, and

00:57:56.310 --> 00:58:01.640
then put them in a position of providing
guidance, and that can really work.

00:58:01.640 --> 00:58:03.960
With that, I'm going to thank you very
much.

00:58:03.960 --> 00:58:08.390
I welcome you to ask questions later, and
enjoy the rest of your reunion weekend.

00:58:08.390 --> 00:58:14.569
&gt;&gt; Thank you.
[APPLAUSE]

00:58:14.569 --> 00:58:16.884
[MUSIC]

00:58:16.884 --> 00:58:19.560
[BLANK_AUDIO]

